test_policy -- control-volume policies: median, blend, hybrid (Nov 2026)
=======================================================================

QUICK START

  sh run_policy_tests.sh [path-to-del_vor]     27 runs, about 8 s
  open summary.html

WHAT IS TESTED

  del_vor's cv keyword (design document, section 9): where each
  cell's dual points go.

    voronoi   circumcenters (alpha = 1; the default)
    median    centroids (alpha = 0); face points at face centroids
    blend     one alpha in every cell (keyword alpha)
    hybrid    the adaptive hybrid: stage A puts the point of each
              boundary cell whose circumcenter lies outside on its
              boundary facet (closed form); stage B lowers alpha cell
              by cell until no coefficient is negative; alpha = 1
              elsewhere

  One case per line of cases.txt, each a run with -stor, -alpha_file
  (alpha and stage per cell) and -report. Meshes: LaGriT's Poisson
  meshes (test_poisson_lagrit), test_dfn's network, test_parity's
  small network, lanl/voronoi's graded cube, test_delaunay's UTM and
  flipped meshes (made by their generators), and this suite's two
  random meshes (inputs/: random points in a square and a cube,
  connected by LaGriT; their many obtuse boundary cells need stage B),
  and LaGriT's hex-to-tet demo (inputs/hextet5.inp: 3 materials, not
  Delaunay; Carl, Nov 11).

WHERE THE ANSWERS COME FROM

  check_policy.py computes the dual geometry itself, in Python, from
  the definitions (its header says how), with each run's alpha from
  its alpha file: cell point P = G + alpha (C - G); face point the
  same blend with alpha(f) = min over the face's two tets; pieces as
  the dual segment (triangles) or polygon m -> F -> P -> F (tets);
  circumcenters from their own small linear systems. It shares no
  code with del_vor.

WHAT IS CHECKED

  geometry      every A/d and volume in the .stor equals the Python
                geometry with the run's alpha, to 1e-11 of the
                largest; pairs left out are zero-area
  balance       the volumes add up to the mesh volume (area), 1e-13;
                both sums exact (math.fsum: a running sum of many
                equal cell volumes, as in a mesh made from a grid,
                rounds the same way each time, 9e-14 on hextet5)
  negative      no negative coefficient where the case must have none
                (the hybrid gate, 2D median); else measured; always
                equal to the report's last row
  exact         median: each vertex volume is 1/3 (triangles) or 1/4
                (tets) of each of its cells, 1e-13
  linear        2D and networks: blend 0.5 = (Voronoi + median) / 2
                to 1e-13 (a coefficient is linear in the cell point)
  stage A       every alpha stage A set equals the closed form
                b / (b - lam), b = 1/(d+1), lam the circumcenter's
                barycentric coordinate at the boundary facet; alpha 1
                where neither stage acted; none below alpha_min
  interior      cells with no boundary face keep alpha 1 (or, where
                stage B ran, how many it lowered and how many cell
                layers in: MEASURED)
  closed        3D: the vector areas around each interior vertex add
                up to zero (the face points are single-valued), 1e-12
  same          where nothing needs fixing, the hybrid's .stor equals
                the Voronoi one byte for byte (after the header)
  utm           the hybrid at (7e5, 3.2e6, 1.5e3) gives the alpha
                and A/d of the origin run to 1e-6 (the moved
                coordinates themselves differ at ~2e-8), and lowers
                the same cells
  warning       alpha_min 0.5 and hybrid_iter 5: a WARNING, exit 0,
                files written, the negatives left reported exactly
  advisory      median on a mesh that is not Delaunay (flip3d,
                hextet5) runs (exit 0); the hybrid on it stops (exit
                1, no .stor)

FOUND (Nov 10 2026)

  - Stage A alone removes all 75 negative coefficients of LaGriT's
    Poisson mesh (108 boundary tets lowered, smallest alpha 0.141).
  - On random points connected by LaGriT, stage A fixes the boundary
    but makes interior coefficients negative (moved points break the
    interior cancellation); stage B removes them: 2D 3 passes, 3D 18
    passes, lowering cells up to 3 (2D) and 6 (3D) layers in.
  - blend 0.5 on the Poisson mesh has 21 negative coefficients
    (Voronoi 75, median 0): in 3D a coefficient is not linear in
    alpha, since cell and face points both move.
  - Median gave no negative coefficient on any mesh here, 3D included.
  - LaGriT's hex-to-tet demo mesh (3 materials, wave-shaped): 3432
    facets not Delaunay; Voronoi coefficients would have 4706 negative
    ones (counted by this suite's geometry; LaGriT's dump / stor
    reports the same 4706). Median: none, and the volumes exact. The
    hybrid stops on it, as it needs a Delaunay mesh.

PICTURES (logs/)

  NAME.svg   the mesh colored by alpha per cell (tets: the boundary
             faces, and a dot for each interior cell with alpha < 1),
             or by material when alpha is the same everywhere (median,
             blend); red: edges negative with Voronoi; black: edges
             still negative in that run

